    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d496">

                <pb facs="0452" n="336" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg336"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 83800/787.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Port au Prince</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-01-30">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 30, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>City quiet as a result of landing of marines and seamen. The <hi
                        rendition="#italic">South Carolina</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Montana</hi> jointly have about 120 men ashore. Germans have 70 men ashore,
                    and the British cruiser will land men on her arrival to-day. French war vessel
                    is on the way here from Mexico. Presence of foreign war vessels and the landing
                    of marines promises to accomplish the peaceful choice of a President, which
                    seemed to be impossible a few days ago.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
